      "seed": 1668300334,
      "version": 257,
      "versionNonce": 214531314,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1794413401593,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "7. Bottlenecks and failiures : \n    1. The Ranking FunnelA heavy Deep Learning Recommendation Model (DLRM) takes ~5ms to score a single user-video pair. You cannot score millions\nof videos per user request. The system relies on a funnel:\n    i. Candidate Generation (Recall): Reduces millions of videos to ~2,000. Uses Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) search via FAISS. It looks at the\nuser's embedding vector and finds the closest video vectors. It also pulls in social graph content (creators they follow) and global trending videos.\n    ii. Lightweight Ranking: A fast, shallow model (e.g., Logistic Regression or Gradient Boosted Trees) scores the 2,000 candidates based on basic\nfeatures (e.g., user age, video language, basic CTR) and trims the list to ~500.\n    iii. Heavy Ranking (Precision): The DLRM takes the 500 candidates and joins them with massive, real-time features from the Online Feature Store\n(e.g., \"Has this user liked 3 fitness videos in the last hour?\", \"What is the video's exact completion rate in the last 5 minutes?\"). It predicts the\nprobability of multiple actions ($P(like)$, $P(watch\\_time > 5s)$, $P(skip)$) and calculates a final utility score.\n    iv. Re-ranking (Heuristics): The top 50 videos are shuffled to enforce business rules. If 5 videos in a row have the exact same audio track or\ncreator, they are separated. This is also where exploration (injecting 1 or 2 totally random/new videos) occurs.\n\n    2. Avoiding the Filter Bubble (Exploration vs. Exploitation)If a user watches a cat video, the model will naturally recommend 100 more cat videos. To\nprevent this filter bubble, the Re-ranking stage uses an Epsilon-Greedy approach or Thompson Sampling. The system deliberately dedicates 10-15% of\nthe feed to \"Exploration\" content (videos slightly outside the user's known embeddings). If the user engages with the exploratory content, their\nembedding vector shifts, expanding their interests dynamically.\n\n    3. The \"Viral Ad/Video\" Problem (10M Views in an Hour)When a creator posts a video that goes hyper-viral, the system breaks in two ways:\n    i. Feature Store Hot Keys: Millions of inference requests try to read the viral video's features from Redis simultaneously, crashing the shard.\nSolution: The heavy ranking service maintains a localized, in-memory LRU cache of features for the top 0.1% most active videos.\n    ii. Feedback Loop Lag: If the model only trains offline every 24 hours, it won't know the video is viral until tomorrow. Solution: Flink stream\nprocessors consume Kafka events and constantly update the video's moving averages (CTR, watch time) in the Online Feature Store every few seconds,\nallowing the Heavy Ranker to exploit the virality instantly.\n\n    4. Measuring Success (Ranking Changes)You measure success using an A/B testing framework.\n    i. Short-term metrics (1-7 days): Watch time per session, interaction rates (likes/shares), and scroll depth. These are highly sensitive to ranking\ntweaks.\n    ii. Long-term metrics (Horizon of 2-3 months): Session frequency (does the user open the app more days per week?) and 30-day retention. If a\nranking change optimizes for clickbait, short-term watch time will spike, but long-term retention will plummet as user trust degrades.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "7. Bottlenecks and failiures : \n    1. The Ranking FunnelA heavy Deep Learning Recommendation Model (DLRM) takes ~5ms to score a single user-video pair. You cannot score millions of videos per user request. The system relies on a funnel:\n    i. Candidate Generation (Recall): Reduces millions of videos to ~2,000. Uses Approximate Nearest Neighbor (ANN) search via FAISS. It looks at the user's embedding vector and finds the closest video vectors. It also pulls in social graph content (creators they follow) and global trending videos.\n    ii. Lightweight Ranking: A fast, shallow model (e.g., Logistic Regression or Gradient Boosted Trees) scores the 2,000 candidates based on basic features (e.g., user age, video language, basic CTR) and trims the list to ~500.\n    iii. Heavy Ranking (Precision): The DLRM takes the 500 candidates and joins them with massive, real-time features from the Online Feature Store (e.g., \"Has this user liked 3 fitness videos in the last hour?\", \"What is the video's exact completion rate in the last 5 minutes?\"). It predicts the probability of multiple actions ($P(like)$, $P(watch\\_time > 5s)$, $P(skip)$) and calculates a final utility score.\n    iv. Re-ranking (Heuristics): The top 50 videos are shuffled to enforce business rules. If 5 videos in a row have the exact same audio track or creator, they are separated. This is also where exploration (injecting 1 or 2 totally random/new videos) occurs.\n\n    2. Avoiding the Filter Bubble (Exploration vs. Exploitation)If a user watches a cat video, the model will naturally recommend 100 more cat videos. To prevent this filter bubble, the Re-ranking stage uses an Epsilon-Greedy approach or Thompson Sampling. The system deliberately dedicates 10-15% of the feed to \"Exploration\" content (videos slightly outside the user's known embeddings). If the user engages with the exploratory content, their embedding vector shifts, expanding their interests dynamically.\n\n    3. The \"Viral Ad/Video\" Problem (10M Views in an Hour)When a creator posts a video that goes hyper-viral, the system breaks in two ways:\n    i. Feature Store Hot Keys: Millions of inference requests try to read the viral video's features from Redis simultaneously, crashing the shard. Solution: The heavy ranking service maintains a localized, in-memory LRU cache of features for the top 0.1% most active videos.\n    ii. Feedback Loop Lag: If the model only trains offline every 24 hours, it won't know the video is viral until tomorrow. Solution: Flink stream processors consume Kafka events and constantly update the video's moving averages (CTR, watch time) in the Online Feature Store every few seconds, allowing the Heavy Ranker to exploit the virality instantly.\n\n    4. Measuring Success (Ranking Changes)You measure success using an A/B testing framework.\n    i. Short-term metrics (1-7 days): Watch time per session, interaction rates (likes/shares), and scroll depth. These are highly sensitive to ranking tweaks.\n    ii. Long-term metrics (Horizon of 2-3 months): Session frequency (does the user open the app more days per week?) and 30-day retention. If a ranking change optimizes for clickbait, short-term watch time will spike, but long-term retention will plummet as user trust degrades.",
